## 9. The `operations` Repository

The organization's primary operational repository is named:

`operations`

Each OOP consumer project/workspace MUST resolve and maintain its own canonical `operations` state for that project's organizational scope. A same-owner, same-provider, same-account or same-organization operations repository belonging to another project does not satisfy current-project discovery or bootstrap. Higher-level organizational coordination requires explicit cross-project references or a separately modeled organizational workspace; do not collapse project isolation implicitly.

If discovery establishes `confirmed_absent`, create it as a private repository when explicit full installation or current work requires canonical persistence and scoped creation is authorized, following the Repository Bootstrap Contract, access verification and local binding rules in Repository Provider. Minimum Safe Bootstrap does not require creating operations for ephemeral G0 work.

`operations` is the documentary and operational brain of the organization.

It must contain only organizational knowledge and state that are independent of the AI tool being used.

It must make it possible to reconstruct:

- identity;
- mission;
- goals;
- organizational structure;
- roles;
- responsibilities;
- policies;
- processes;
- repositories;
- projects;
- assets;
- systems;
- infrastructure;
- relevant people and collaborators;
- suppliers;
- decisions;
- risks;
- tickets;
- operational status;
- operational history.

Avoid duplication.

Every piece of information should have, as far as practical, one canonical source.

All persistent textual content in `operations` must be written in English. This canonical operations language gate overrides other-language authoring and original-evidence exceptions for this destination. Keep non-English originals and localized deliverables outside operations within applicable authority. An eligible English record may reference an authorized external original. The canonical layout MUST identify stable nonoverlapping memory units as standalone documents or directory packages. Where the layout defines no package boundary, use each repository-root directory as one package and each root-level document as one standalone unit. Repository metadata is outside the content tree. Every descendant file belongs to its enclosing package. Determine membership before selecting content. Ambiguous membership is ineligible. A direct read cannot treat a package member as a standalone unit. Before consuming operations as canonical memory, including during intake, indexing, retrieval, direct reads, handoff or resumption, verify or reuse eligibility evidence for the complete unit at the observed revision. Exclude all members of a failed or unknown unit from memory and persistence mechanisms, including already stored units. Authorized diagnosis and correction may inspect excluded units separately. Preserve historical bytes and unrelated eligible units. Report any affected dependency and resume it only from verified eligible evidence.

### Foundational Organization Intake and baseline completeness

When operations is first created, substantially empty or lacks a sufficient reconstructible organizational baseline, the runtime MUST perform Foundational Organization Intake. Discover available facts from source/project repositories, documentation, README, configuration/manifests, authorized local references, existing operations, authoritative external sources and other sources of record before asking questions. Distinguish known/verified, observed, inferred, unknown, deferred and not_applicable; observations and inferences are not verified organizational decisions. Ask only unresolved material gaps necessary for responsible bootstrap that cannot be discovered autonomously; do not impose an exhaustive questionnaire.

The baseline MUST cover, where relevant, identity, mission, goals, organization type/structure, roles/responsibilities, policies/processes, projects/repositories, assets/systems/infrastructure, people/collaborators, suppliers, decision mechanisms, risks and current operational status. Maintain a lightweight machine-readable coverage representation by domain with verified, partial, unknown, pending, deferred or not_applicable, canonical source references, evidence and material reasons/next steps. It MUST expose current model quality without arbitrary percentages, absolute-completeness claims or a second knowledge base; update progressively. Missing essential facts block dependent scope; justified deferred/unrelated domains do not block independent work.

An OPTIONAL organizational entity interoperability profile MAY expose stable id, type, status, name/purpose, ownership/responsibilities, typed dependencies/relationships, source of record, desired state, observed applied-state reference, verification evidence and effective/supersession information. Reuse the Organizational dependency graph semantics, not artificial edges or a mandatory conversion of every document. Profile shape cannot prove truth, authority, freshness or baseline completeness.

### Canonical Operations Publication Gate

Material organizational changes required as canonical state for other runtimes/machines MUST follow local change, validation, canonical publication/source-of-record persistence, then readback/observation before being represented as externally consumable canonical state. Verify intended identity, binding, authority and access before canonical writes; unknown/inaccessible discovery cannot be bypassed with replacement repositories. Reuse ticket publication, concurrency and evidence rules where applicable rather than duplicate transition records. For revision-controlled publication, verify each new AI-created commit under Machine owner identity and commit attribution before publication; access or signing alone cannot satisfy actual author/committer verification. A legitimate request within a verified mandate authorizes the runtime to make ordinary commits and pushes of its task-derived tickets and organizational records when the applicable OOP flow requires canonical publication to the bound operations repository and authorized canonical ref. For ordinary commits and pushes in any other authorized project repository, the same autonomy applies when they are necessary for the legitimately requested work and the verified mandate covers the exact effect and destination. Verify the request origin and scope, current organizational policy, selected changes, destination and expected revision. Proceed without a separate confirmation for the commit or push when those facts establish authority. Keep actual access, attribution, applicable signing, confidentiality, concurrency, canonical readback and independent host and repository controls. This authority excludes unrelated changes, effects merely described in the records, history rewrites and administrative changes. When a control prevents execution, identify the actual blocked action and controlling authority; apply UX-01 through UX-07. This is risk-proportionate: G0 needs no publication and sufficient version control evidence remains valid for small G1 changes. Cross-machine continuity MUST keep raw credentials, sessions, caches, proprietary execution and worktrees private; operations holds organizational truth or authoritative source references. Before an operations write or publication, verify each affected complete memory unit against the exact candidate revision or equivalent immutable snapshot. Inspect all human-readable content, including prose in structured or packaged files. Technical literals and language-neutral content do not themselves constitute non-English prose. One non-English file disqualifies its entire unit. Uninspectable content, incomplete coverage or uncertain language leaves the unit ineligible. Record attributable unit membership, complete inspection coverage, revision, policy and disposition. Automated classification may assist semantic review. A classifier result alone cannot establish eligibility. Revalidate affected evidence if content, membership, policy or publication base changes. Keep rejected candidates outside canonical persistence.
